      "source": [
        "# **Section I — Data Overview**\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "**Dataset — Student Performance Report** (https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/320/student+performance)\n",
        "\n",
        "**About**\n",
        "\n",
        "The Student Performance Report is a dataset that contains information mainly focused on academic performance and the factors in a student's life that affect it. The factors reflect a wide range, from personal and familial influences to academic related variables.\n",
        "\n",
        "Some of these factors are factual while others are claimed or assumed. What this (final) project does is seperate the factual from the claimed/assumed through data analysis tools such as Wrangling & Predictive Modeling.\n",
        "\n",
        "In real life contexts, these datasets are used to identify struggling students early on, and the factors that affect performance, in order to personalize learning paths, improve teaching strategies, guide educational politices and help specialists (counsellors, tutors, mental help) intervene before students fall far too behind, using simple metrics (discussed later in section). Although this is important for every academic context, data driven decisions like these are especially helpful for high-poverty, low-graduate rate schools because they can highly influence a student's life, positvely but also negatively, if not taken seriously. We'll discuss this further in the last section of this report.\n",
        "\n",
        "For now, however, we'll dive deeper into the technical aspect of the data.\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "\n",
        "**Where Does The Data Come Frome?**\n",
        "\n",
        "This particular (originally collected by Paulo Cortez and Alice Silva) dataset comes from UC Irvine, a Machine Learning Repository website that maintains about 700 datasets (!) in order to donate and find datasets used by millions of people all around the world! This was last updated on the webiste on 11/26/2014, and describes the dataset as \"predicting student performance in secondary education\".\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "\n",
        "**Why Did I Select This Particular Dataset?**\n",
        "\n",
        "There's two versions of the dataset availabile, divided by subject; one in Mathematics and the other in Portugese. I've selected Mathematics because of it's general technical difficulty, which may help find deeper root causes than the other dataset which has a language context as its subject. In other words, the problem I see (with the Portugese dataset) is that people who may have a strong cultural base in Portugese may have higher grades than most, while failing other courses. Mathematics on the other hand is a subject that doesn't come naturally to most, irrespective of the context they grew up in; it's something that people have to learn (even if they have natural technical ability as a gift). Students could also choose not to take a course on the Portugese language, and that narrows down the dataset. And so, the Mathematics dataset can help find deeper root causes in a more general context rather than a specific one.\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "\n",
        "**What Is Contained In The Data?**\n",
        "\n",
        "A few characteristics of the dataset is that it's,\n",
        "* Multivariate\n",
        "* Focuses on Social Science (in an academic context)\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "\n",
        "The metrics used to collect most of the data below aren't high-level, they're either plainly academic (GPA, Study Hours) that are calculated by simple proportions or are just factual (Father's Education, Romantic Interest).\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "\n",
        "Here's all the columnns from the dataset and what they mean:\n",
        "* **school**: Student’s school, GP (Gabriel Pereira) or MS (Mousinho da Silveira)\n",
        "* **sex**: Student’s sex, F (female) or M (male)\n",
        "* **age**: Student’s age, numeric (15–22)\n",
        "* **addres**s: Home address type, U (urban) or R (rural)\n",
        "* **famsize**: Family size, LE3 which means Less than Equal to (≤3) or GT3, which means Greater than Three to (>3)\n",
        "* **Pstatus**: Parents’ cohabitation status, T (together) or A (apart)\n",
        "* **Medu**: Mother’s education level, 0 (none) to 4 (higher education)\n",
        "* **Fedu**: Father’s education level, 0 (none) to 4 (higher education)\n",
        "* **Mjob**: Mother’s occupation -> teacher, health, services, at_home, other\n",
        "* **Fjob**: Father’s occupation -> teacher, health, services, at_home, other\n",
        "* **reason**: Reason for choosing the school -> home, reputation, course, other\n",
        "* **guardian**: Student’s guardian, mother, father, other\n",
        "* **traveltime**: Travel time to school, 1 (<15 min) to 4 (>1 hour)\n",
        "* **studytime**: Weekly study time, 1 (<2 hrs) to 4 (>10 hrs)\n",
        "* **failures**: Number of past class failures, numeric (1–3, 4 if ≥4)\n",
        "* **schoolsup**: Extra school support, yes or no\n",
        "* **famsup**: Family educational support, yes or no\n",
        "* **paid**: Extra paid classes for the subject, yes or no\n",
        "* **activities**: Participation in extracurricular activities, yes or no\n",
        "* **nursery**: Attended nursery school, yes or no\n",
        "* **higher**: Desire to pursue higher education, yes or no\n",
        "* **internet**: Internet access at home, yes or no\n",
        "* **romantic**: In a romantic relationship, yes or no\n",
        "* **famrel**: Quality of family relationships, 1 (very bad) to 5 (excellent)\n",
        "* **freetime**: Free time after school, 1 (very low) to 5 (very high)\n",
        "* **goout**: Frequency of going out with friends, 1 (very low) to 5 (very high)\n",
        "* **Dalc**: Workday alcohol consumption, 1 (very low) to 5 (very high)\n",
        "* **Walc**: Weekend alcohol consumption, 1 (very low) to 5 (very high)\n",
        "* **health**: Current health status, 1 (very bad) to 5 (very good)\n",
        "* **absences**: Number of school absences, numeric (0–93)\n",
        "* **G1:** First period grade, numeric (0–20)\n",
        "* **G2**: Second period grade, numeric (0–20)\n",
        "* **G3**: Final grade (target variable), numeric (0–20)\n",
        "\n",
        "A lot of these column names, especially without context, aren't very descriptive to a non-technical person, and aren't helpful to a technical person either. We'll deal with this in the Wrangling section.\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "\n",
        "**Prior Work Analysis**\n",
        "\n",
        "* Narrowed Factors\n",
        "\n",
        "Something that the dataset does very well is narrow down factors that are helpful for the context. What I mean is that the dataset does not have unnecessary columns (like Height or Ethnicity) that do not have any relation to academic performance. What this helps with greatly is narrowing the factors further down: I know, now, that all of these factors are influential, it's just trying to delve deeper on which factors are more influential than others. That's what I'll be doing in the EDA section of this project, picking up variables that I think (and the data supports) are highly influential.\n",
        "\n",
        "* Specificity\n",
        "\n",
        "Another thing that the dataset does well is that it uses metrics (albeit simple) for each factor. For example, Family Size isn't just \"Big\" or \"Small\", it's specific. Specificity helps the credibility of the dataset and the conclusion of my project.\n",
        "\n",
        "* Less Parallel Variable Relationship\n",
        "\n",
        "That being said, there are a few limitations just as well. No single variable perfectly separates students who pass from those who fail. Even students with high study time may fail, and students with limited support may still succeed. This overlap underscores the need for a model where multiple features are considered simultaneously to capture interacting effects, not just a few. We'll reflect on this limitation, again, in the last section of the report.\n",
        "\n",
        "* Messy Naming Structure\n",
        "\n",
        "Another limitation that I found was technical in nature: the column names are highly ambigious, some more than others. A non technical person would have a hard time understanding the columns as well as the metrics used to measure it (even if they're simple metrics).\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "\n",
        "**What Is The Broad Question For The Dataset?**\n",
        "\n",
        "Now, with the dataset, after explaning what the origional data means, the question is this: what am I trying to do with it?\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "\n",
        "I'll answer the broader question in two ways,\n",
        "\n",
        "* Technical Application\n",
        "\n",
        "This is asking the analytical question of *can we predict whether a student passes the course (defined as achieving a final grade above a passing threshold) using a subset of demographic, family, and behavioral features from the Student Performance Dataset?* This is something that we'll cover through most of the report.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "* Real-World Application\n",
        "\n",
        "This deals with the real life factors that are associated with *whether a student passes a mathematics course, and how well can those factors be used to predict academic success?* and the real world consequences associated with it. Although we'll cover this a little in each section, most of it will be covered and summarized in the last section.\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "\n",
        "To address the technical question, however, we frame the problem as a binary classification task. Rather than attempting to predict an exact final grade, we focus on whether a student passes or does not pass the course. This framing better reflects how grades are often used in practice and avoids over-interpreting small numerical differences in scores.\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "<br>"
      ],
