        "# **Section IV — Moldeling**\n",
        "\n",
        "# IV(A) Overview\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "\n",
        "**Target Variable Used For Prediction**\n",
        "\n",
        "The target variable for this project is **Passed Final Grade**, a binary indicator of whether a student passed the course. I created this variable during the Wrangling stage of the project. I chose this variable because it represents a clear outcome and also is binary which is a central focus of the predictive modeling framework I'm about to use (Logistic & Decision Trees). Framing the problem this way allows me to answer the primary question of *\"can I predict whether a student will pass based on information available before the final exam?\"*\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "\n",
        "**Features Used for Prediction**\n",
        "* Weekly Study Time: Histogram showed that students who passed were more concentrated in higher study-time categories.\n",
        "* Past Class Failures: Boxplots showed that students who failed the course had a higher number of past failures, while most passing students had zero.\n",
        "* School Absences: Boxplot revealed higher variability and more extreme absences among students who did not pass.\n",
        "* Family Support: Grouped bar chart showed that students reporting family support had a higher number of passing outcomes\n",
        "\n",
        "I chose these variables because they balance academic, behavioral, and social factors, reflecting a multidimensional approach.\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "\n",
        "**Measuring Accuracy**\n",
        "\n",
        "Model performance is measured using classification accuracy, defined as the proportion of students whose pass/fail outcome is predicted correctly.\n",
        "\n",
        "Accuracy is an appropriate metric for this project because,\n",
        "* Because the target variable is binary\n",
        "* The class distribution is not extreme (67% vs. 33%)\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, an accuracy of 0.72 means that the model correctly predicts the final outcome for 72 out of every 100 students, which can be directly compared to the 67% baseline from the majority-class (dummy) model.\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "\n",
        "**Validation Method**\n",
        "\n",
        "To evaluate model performance reliably, 5-fold Stratified Cross-Validation was used.\n",
        "\n",
        "This validation approach was chosen because:\n",
        "* Stratification ensures that each fold preserves the original pass/fail proportion (approximately 67% pass, 33% fail), preventing misleading (caused by unbalanced splits).\n",
        "* Cross-validation allows every data point to be used for both training and validation, which is especially important given the moderate dataset size (395 students).\n",
        "* Provides a distribution of accuracy scores (not just a single accuracy value), allowing assessment of both model performance and stability.\n",
        "\n",
        "The mean accuracy across folds is used as the conclusion metric, while the standard deviation helps indicate how sensitive the model is to different data splits.\n",
        "<br>\n",
