        "<br>\n",
        "\n",
        "**Predictive Model Used**\n",
        "\n",
        "For the basic predictive model, I used Logistic Regression, primarily because the target variable, Passed Final Grade, **is binary**. Other reasons that I used this model are,\n",
        "* **Interpretability**: Logistic Regression allows direct interpretation of how each feature affects the likelihood of passing\n",
        "* **Appropriateness for Baseline:** Interpretable model establishes a strong baseline before moving to the updated model, which is Decision Trees.\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "\n",
        "**Features Used**: As stated above, Weekly Study Time, Past Class Failure, School Absences & Family Support\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "\n",
        "**Hyperparameters**: None at this stage, yet. The goal is to establish a baseline for now, not maximize accuracy.\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "\n",
        "**Validation**: Explained in IV(A)\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "\n",
        "**Expected Performace**\n",
        "* **Above-Baseline Performance**: Performs better than a naive baseline, which only skews towards either \"pass\" or \"fail\" but doesn't estimate a decent accuracy. I expect that this model is more unbiased. The dummy classifier already sits at 67%, so my expectation is that the model has to beat ~67% by a clear margin to prove it learned something meaningful.\n",
        "* **Feature/Variable Causation**: I expect that Past Class Failures should be one of the strongest signals. It’s basically a summary of prior academic struggle, so I expect the model to learn a strong negative association (more failures leads to lower odds of passing). For Weekly Study Time, I expect that this variable should push the probability upward becauase higher study times means higher odds of passing. I also expect School Absences to be a strong negative predictor, and Family Support to be neutral because it's a contexual variable.\n",
