        "# **Section V — Conclusion & Summary**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Findings**\n",
        "\n",
        "This project analyzed factors associated with whether a student passes a final grade in a Mathematics course context, using a combination of wrangling, exploratory data analysis, and predictive modeling.\n",
        "\n",
        "The goal was to answer **2 questions**,\n",
        "* *How well can we predict a student passing a (Mathematics) course?*\n",
        "* *What factors are most influential in hindering a student's ability to pass the course?*\n",
        "\n",
        "The target variable, **Passed Final Grade**, was constructed from the final grade (G3), allowing the problem to be framed as a binary classification task, without allowing data leakage.\n",
        "\n",
        "Across both EDA, there were a few features that popped up as influential, these were **Weekly Study Time, Absences, Past Class Failures & Family Support**. For modeling, I tried using both a basic Logistic Regression Model as well as a Decision Tree Model, but the former ended up being a better fit because it had smoother folds as well as stable variation, while the Decision Tree ended up overfitting features.\n",
        "\n",
        "Through modeling, however, **Past Class Failures** consistently emerged as the strongest predictor of passing. Visualizations supported this, by showing a clear separation in outcomes between students with zero prior failures and those with one or more. I then examined the Logistic Regression model to analyze which feature had the largest coefficient magnitude and the strongest odds ratio effect; the answer was in order **Past Class Failures, School Absences, Family Support and at last, Weekly Study Time**.\n",
        "\n",
        "The logistic regression model achieved a mean cross-validated accuracy of approximately 72%, outperforming a dummy baseline model and providing reasonable predictive performance for a simple, interpretable model.\n",
        "\n",
        "All in all, the findings suggest that **academic history and stability matter more than short-term behavioral changes when predicting final outcomes. That however, can be a very perspective opinion**. We are going to discuss this in the perspective limitations below.\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "\n",
        "**Limitations**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Technical**\n",
        "* **Limited Features & Causation**: The model uses only a limited subset of features and relies on logistic regression and assumes that all the features have a linear relationship. This limits predictive power compared to more complex models, because although we can find out the intensity of a feature (like which feature is most influential versus least influential), it's difficult to apply causation because that would mean analyzing more than 1-2 factors simultaneously, which would result in a much more complex model\n",
        "* **Small Dataset**: The data is a small subset of students, and it may not reflect broader challenges faced by students in passing a course.\n",
        "* **Target Variable Intensity**: The target variable was constructed using a threshold on G3, which simplifies performance into a binary pass/fail. While this is useful for modeling, it removes information about how well students passed or failed (did a student just pass, or just fail?).\n",
        "* **Behavioral Pattern Prediction**: Because all features were measured at a single point in time, the model cannot capture changes in behavior or improvement over time.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Perspective**\n",
        "\n",
        "* **High Risk of Oversimplyfying Data in Context**: From a broader perspective, the dataset does not capture important contextual factors such as teaching quality, learning disabilities, mental health, or socioeconomic pressures beyond basic indicators. As a result, the model risks oversimplifying student success into a small number of measurable variables. Think of the feature, Family Support, for example. Our findings concluded that reported Family Support is associated with lower odds of passing a course. Psychologically however, it is more than clear that Family Support can significantly and positively affect a student's ability to perfom well academically (source: https://americanspcc.org/how-parent-support-can-enhance-student-well-being-and-success/). Why the mismatch then? What could be an explanation is that, for students who reported having Family Support, there are other factors in play that hinder them from performing at their best, academically. But those factors either aren't measured or aren't reported, which may make certain important factors seem as if they reduce the odds of a student's chance of success when in reality, they do.\n",
        "* **Looking at Data as Numbers, not Students**: This one is as equally important as the first one. When we use data like this and rely solely on technology to make patterns for us, instead of stepping in with the discernment and empathy that God has uniquely blessed humans with, we risk looking at children as data points and not God's creation, all of whom have unique talents that they're blessed with. When we solely use data, we are looking for outcomes, and sometimes, that can force a person to push their own narrative, which in turn can bring heavy bias into a classroom setting. It's like the analogy of the \"Marriage Counseling Couple\"; if they're determined to have a divorce, they'll eventually get it. If you keep looking at a academic struglling child through what the data tells you (without human intervention, like academic intervention or psychological help), you're bound to see them as \"failing\". In contexts where there's high poverty and low number of opportunities presented (competitive resources), incomplete data can significantly reduce the odds of a student's success, all because the model couldn't fully predict if a student would have enough to succeed academically.\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "\n",
        "**Ethics & Future Direction**\n",
        "* **Technically**: Many of the issues for the findings could be reduced by improving how the data and models are constructed. One way to address the limitation of linear assumptions would be to introduce models that can capture interactions between variables (for example, how family support and past failures together affect outcomes), rather than treating each feature independently. Also, collecting longitudinal data—tracking students across multiple semesters instead of a single snapshot would allow the model to capture behavioral change and improvement over time, rather than assuming student performance is static. This'd actually help the model to \"learn\". At the same time, using a continuous outcome variable (such as final grade rather than pass/fail) could also preserve more information without fitting in noise.\n",
        "* **Perspectively**: The most important step is recognizing that predictive models should be used as diagnostic tools, not decision-makers. The findings of this project show strong associations but these patterns should be interpreted as signals for intervention, not labels of ability or potential for students. Both engineers and teachers should be taught (through certifications, programs, HR trainings, seminars) to recognize biases and avoid oversimplifying students into numbers. Schools and Colleges should also support earlier tutoring, counseling, or academic resources rather than justify reduced expectations (implicit bias). Educators should also encourage a culture where asking for help is seen as normal and not a weakness. Justice should be heavily prioritized, especially as Christain institutions.\n",
